<section class="section" id="executive-overview">
    <h2><span class="section-num">1</span>Executive Overview</h2>
    <p>
        This report analyzes <strong>{N} monthly crude oil price observations</strong> from
        <strong>January 1970</strong> through <strong>March 2026</strong>, spanning {fmt(years_span, 1)} years
        of global energy market history. Over this period, crude oil transformed from a low-cost,
        stable commodity priced near <strong>${fmt(first_price)}/bbl</strong> to a volatile, geopolitically
        sensitive asset that has traded as high as <strong>${fmt(max_price)}/bbl</strong> (July 2008).
    </p>
    <p>
        The dataset reveals a <strong>compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of {fmt(cagr*100)}%</strong>,
        with the price expanding over <strong>{fmt(max_price/first_price, 0)}x</strong> from start to finish.
        However, this growth has been anything but linear. The time series is characterized by
        violent shocks&mdash;the 1973 OPEC embargo, the 1979 Iranian Revolution, the 2008 speculative
        peak, the 2020 pandemic crash&mdash;separated by extended regimes of relative stability.
    </p>
    <p>
        The <strong>{highest_avg_decade[0]}</strong> recorded the highest average price at
        <strong>${fmt(highest_avg_decade[1]["mean"])}/bbl</strong>, driven by the unprecedented
        2008 super-spike and the sustained $100+ environment of 2011&ndash;2014.
        Overall price volatility, as measured by the standard deviation of monthly prices, stands at
        <strong>${fmt(std_dev)}</strong>&mdash;a figure that underscores the extraordinary dispersion
        of outcomes over the dataset's lifetime.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Key Finding</strong>
        The largest single-month percentage move was <strong>{pct(max_pct_increase["pct_change"], 1)}</strong>
        in <strong>{max_pct_increase["date"].strftime("%B %Y")}</strong>, while the steepest decline was
        <strong>{pct(max_pct_decrease["pct_change"], 1)}</strong> in
        <strong>{max_pct_decrease["date"].strftime("%B %Y")}</strong>. These extremes illustrate the
        asymmetric risk profile inherent in crude oil markets.
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Long-Term Price Trajectory -->
<section class="section" id="long-term-trend">
    <h2><span class="section-num">2</span>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970&ndash;2026)</h2>
    <p>
        The chart below presents the complete monthly crude oil price series from January 1970 to
        March 2026. Key geopolitical and economic events are annotated to contextualize the major
        inflection points. The trajectory broadly divides into five structural phases:
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Phase 1 (1970&ndash;1973):</strong> A period of extreme stability, with prices
        hovering near $1&ndash;$3/bbl under the administered pricing of the Seven Sisters era.<br>
        <strong>Phase 2 (1973&ndash;1985):</strong> The OPEC-driven revolution, with two major
        price shocks lifting crude from $4 to over $39/bbl.<br>
        <strong>Phase 3 (1986&ndash;2003):</strong> A long regime of moderate, range-bound pricing
        ($10&ndash;$35/bbl), punctuated by the Gulf War spike and Asian crisis trough.<br>
        <strong>Phase 4 (2003&ndash;2014):</strong> The commodity super-cycle, peaking at
        ${fmt(max_price)}/bbl in July 2008 and sustaining triple-digit prices through 2014.<br>
        <strong>Phase 5 (2014&ndash;present):</strong> The shale era and beyond, characterized by
        lower equilibrium prices, the COVID shock, the post-pandemic recovery, and the Ukraine
        war-driven spike.
    </p>
