        The decade began with crude at <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['1970s']['min'])}/bbl</strong> and ended
        above <strong>$39/bbl</strong>. The 1973 Arab oil embargo quadrupled prices virtually overnight,
        and the 1979 Iranian Revolution doubled them again. The decade's standard deviation of
        <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['1970s']['std'])}</strong> reflects this extraordinary transformation
        from a stable, low-cost commodity to a geopolitically weaponized resource.
    </p>

    <h3>1980s: Correction and Collapse</h3>
    <p>
        After peaking near $40/bbl in early 1980, prices entered a prolonged decline as demand
        destruction, new non-OPEC supply (North Sea, Alaska), and Saudi Arabia's decision to
        abandon its swing producer role combined to crash prices to below $10/bbl in mid-1986.
        The decade average of <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['1980s']['mean'])}/bbl</strong> masks the
        dramatic collapse from the early-decade highs to the late-decade lows.
    </p>

    <h3>1990s: Range-Bound Stability</h3>
    <p>
        Apart from the brief Gulf War spike to ${fmt(34.50)} in October 1990, the 1990s were
        the most stable decade in the dataset with a mean of <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['1990s']['mean'])}/bbl</strong>
        and the lowest standard deviation among full decades at <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['1990s']['std'])}</strong>.
        The Asian Financial Crisis drove prices to the dataset's post-1970s low of
        <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['1990s']['min'])}/bbl</strong> in December 1998.
    </p>

    <h3>2000s: The Super-Cycle</h3>
    <p>
        Surging demand from China and other emerging economies, combined with supply constraints,
        propelled the decade's average to <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['2000s']['mean'])}/bbl</strong>.
        The decade encompasses both the all-time peak of <strong>${fmt(max_price)}/bbl</strong> (July 2008)
        and the subsequent crash to $41.34 during the Global Financial Crisis, producing
        the highest decade-level standard deviation of <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['2000s']['std'])}</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3>2010s: Shale, Oversupply, and Rebalancing</h3>
    <p>
        The 2010s carried the highest mean of any decade at
        <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['2010s']['mean'])}/bbl</strong>, buoyed by the
        $100+ environment of 2011&ndash;2014. The US shale revolution then fundamentally altered the
        supply landscape, contributing to the 2014&ndash;2016 price collapse that saw crude fall below
        $30/bbl. Prices recovered into the $50&ndash;$75 range by decade's end.
    </p>

    <h3>2020s: Pandemic, Recovery, and Normalization</h3>
    <p>
        The decade opened with the COVID-19 demand shock crashing prices to
        <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['2020s']['min'])}/bbl</strong> in April 2020.
        The subsequent recovery was turbocharged by Russia's invasion of Ukraine, which drove
        prices to <strong>${fmt(116.80)}/bbl</strong> in June 2022. Since then, prices have
        progressively normalized, with the most recent observations (early 2026) in the
        $60&ndash;$88 range. The decade's average stands at <strong>${fmt(decade_stats['2020s']['mean'])}/bbl</strong>
        across {decade_stats['2020s']['count']} observations to date.
    </p>
</section>

