<section class="section" id="outlook-and-conclusions">
    <h2><span class="section-num">6</span>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>

    <h3>Recent Trajectory (2023&ndash;2026)</h3>
    <p>
        After the Ukraine war-driven spike of mid-2022, crude oil prices have entered a
        normalizing trend. The most recent 12 months of data show prices fluctuating between
        approximately <strong>$58 and $88/bbl</strong>, with a general downward bias through 2025
        before a partial recovery in early 2026. The March 2026 print of
        <strong>${fmt(last_price)}/bbl</strong> represents a sharp uptick from the preceding months,
        though it remains well below the 2022 highs.
    </p>

    <h3>Structural Takeaways</h3>
    <p>
        <strong>1. Mean reversion is powerful but slow.</strong> Every major price shock in the
        dataset was eventually unwound, but the timeline ranged from months (2020 COVID recovery)
        to over a decade (1980s correction). Mean reversion does not imply predictability.<br><br>
        <strong>2. Supply shocks create regimes, not spikes.</strong> The 1973 embargo, 1979
        revolution, and post-2003 super-cycle each established new, sustained price levels
        rather than temporary dislocations.<br><br>
        <strong>3. The shale revolution permanently altered the supply elasticity.</strong>
        Post-2014, the US shale industry acts as a partial price ceiling, ramping production
        when prices rise and slowing when they fall. This has compressed the upper end of
        the price distribution relative to the 2008 peak.<br><br>
        <strong>4. Volatility clusters.</strong> High-volatility periods (visible in the rolling
        standard deviation chart) tend to persist for 12&ndash;24 months, not weeks. Risk management
        must account for regime-based volatility, not a single constant.
    </p>

    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Note on Forward-Looking Data</strong>
        The dataset extends through March 2026. Observations beyond the current date may
        represent forecasts or projections rather than realized prices. Additionally, all prices
        are in nominal US dollars; no inflation adjustment has been applied, meaning the
        real purchasing-power-adjusted growth is lower than the nominal CAGR reported here.
    </div>
</section>

